notes: V038 is an owner-requested new patch after V037. It keeps the V037/R6 quality-preserving ESP camera firmware and aitl-tcp-jpeg-v1 wire protocol unchanged, while adding a built-in Camera Diagnostics page and backend diagnostic service. One Diagnose action runs staged evidence checks against the currently selected saved ESP camera: repeated direct /status control probes; firmware/camera-readiness/Wi-Fi telemetry checks; a direct ATL1/JPEG stream phase that bypasses the normal PC Studio stream worker; a second direct stream phase with concurrent /status polling; and a normal RemoteCameraManager/RemoteCameraService managed-stream phase. The diagnostic temporarily quiesces the selected stream/simulation when needed, uses the saved camera image settings at a conservative 5 FPS during measurement, then restores the previous saved FPS/settings and connection/simulation state. Results classify likely failures such as control-unreachable, firmware mismatch, camera-not-ready, ESP camera/TCP send stall, HTTP-control/stream contention, PC Studio receiver/session integration, weak Wi-Fi margin, or healthy-now, and expose measured evidence rather than requiring a separate command-line test. No new stable error code, ESP-side inference, UDP transport, permanent camera-profile setting change, or physical/public-road signal authority is introduced. V024 / 0_2_4 remains the owner-confirmed passed baseline until explicit owner acceptance changes it. R2 expands the same V038 Camera Test into a deeper functionality/stability/bottleneck diagnostic: eight control probes with avg/p95/max latency, explicit config/start/stop verification, clean direct JPEG integrity/timing, concurrent-control contention testing, saved-target throughput/headroom testing capped at 15 FPS, deliberate TCP receiver replacement/reconnect verification, and a normal managed-worker phase using per-phase counter deltas rather than lifetime counters. It reports a 0-100 stability score, bottleneck attribution, frame-interval p95/max jitter, throughput, FPS headroom, sequence gaps, reconnect timing, Wi-Fi transition deltas, and separates intentional diagnostic socket-reset send failures from unexpected ESP send failures. R6 integrates the R5 comprehensive transport benchmark into the Camera Diagnostics page with live progress and automatic firmware dispatch. R7 improves the same unaccepted 0_3_8 candidate without changing ESP firmware: after the full R5 matrix it runs a short capture-vs-camera-acquisition timing follow-up, combines ESP last_capture_ms/last_send_ms status samples with observed frame intervals, estimates accounted versus unexplained frame time, ranks the remaining FPS limiter, and exposes the timing evidence in the UI. R7 also fixes benchmark report consistency so a complete but slow stream no longer shows a misleading 100% stability score, an under-capacity target is not reported as sustainable, and single-shot /capture latency is excluded from live-stream peak FPS. R8 adds a focused post-R5 alternative-isolation pass without requiring another ESP firmware flash: it derives the median real streaming JPEG size, sweeps exact synthetic internal-DRAM payloads around that size, compares four PC SO_RCVBUF requests, and runs fast-versus-artificially-throttled receiver draining. The report uses those results together with the already-benchmarked HTTP/MJPEG WiFiClient.write(), ATL1 plain send(), staged DRAM, UDP, WebSocket/RTSP assessments and lower-level raw-lwIP option to distinguish payload-size/common-TCP limits, real-camera/data-path-specific limits, and PC receiver-buffer/backpressure effects. Runtime SO_SNDBUF tuning is not claimed because ESP-IDF/lwIP does not provide it by default. R9 adds a dedicated diagnostic-only ESP firmware and adaptive one-click architecture benchmark. R9 restores the older V035-style two-PSRAM-framebuffer CAMERA_GRAB_LATEST configuration and physically compares manual WiFiServer/WiFiClient MJPEG with esp_http_server/httpd_resp_send_chunk MJPEG, plus a Pi-style FreeRTOS newest-frame producer/cache feeding esp_http_server. It also measures 512 KiB camera-free bulk transfer through esp_http_server and raw WiFiClient with TCP_NODELAY both enabled and disabled, and exposes reset-reason/brownout evidence, RSSI/BSSID/channel, and memory telemetry. The classifier can distinguish a manual-socket sender regression, capture/send coupling, camera/JPEG-specific work, a common ESP/network/AP/PC path bottleneck, or a healthy HTTPD architecture. R9 requires flashing its dedicated benchmark sketch before this architecture comparison; it does not replace the production transport, change stable API envelopes, promote 0_3_8, or change the owner-confirmed 0_2_4 passed baseline.